{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he shall make atonement for her.</b> The expression of atonement (כפרה) applies only to sin. Therefore this offering of a woman who has given birth is newly introduced, like the offering of the nazirite — for what did she sin at the time of birth that the Torah should require her to bring an offering? And if this offering is because she came into danger and was saved from death, it would have been fitting for her to bring a thanksgiving offering; why should she bring a burnt offering and a sin offering?" "<b>And it is possible</b> to explain that this offering is not on account of a sin of her own, but on account of her mother, who was the mother of all living — for had it not been for that sin, a man would have begotten with his wife not by way of lust and desire but by way of pure nature, like the nature of a tree that brings forth its fruit every year without desire. And this woman who gives birth is like her mother her daughter in the act of that sin, for the branches are corrupted through the corruption of the root. Therefore Scripture requires her to bring an offering to atone for that primal sin, for on account of the cause of that sin she was commanded in three commandments, and they are the laws of the menstruant (נדה), חלה, and the lighting of the lamp. And since that sin was first in thought and afterward in deed, for this reason Scripture mentioned in the offering of the woman who has given birth one for a burnt offering and one for a sin offering — corresponding to the sin of thought and the sin of deed. And for this reason it placed the burnt offering before the sin offering, unlike the rest of the offerings, where the sin offering precedes the burnt offering in all of them. And that which we learned in Zevaḥim — 'one for a burnt offering and one for a sin offering' — Scripture did not place it first except for the reading, and the intention is that Scripture did not place the burnt offering before the sin offering to indicate that it is prior in the act. Rather, the reason it placed it first in the reading is for this reason: to allude to the sin of that thought which preceded the sin of the deed. And according to the view of our Rabbis of blessed memory, the sin of the woman who gives birth is that she transgressed an oath — from what they said: at the time she crouches to give birth, she leaps up and swears, 'I will not again have relations with my husband.' And since she swears out of distress, and the oath is not fit to be upheld because she is subservient to her husband, therefore Scripture requires her to bring an offering to atone for the sin of thought."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:10.432164853Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}